is slide uh  many years ago in the early 1940s a very  famous author by the name of Isaac  Asimov wrote a series of books called  iRobot  and this series of books depicted uh  robots with intelligence who one could  actually communicate with and at that  time certainly that this was not uh  possible in reality but it was uh  something that was just in the mind of  uh the readers and also Isaac Asimov so  it was just fiction at the time  in the 1940s also uh in the early 40s  there was a major war going on World War  II and uh the uh German uh military had  this code called the Enigma code which  uh carried important information uh that  was uh critical for moving the uh uh  War forward for the German army but uh  this person here on the bottom right  this is uh Dr Alan Turing was able to  use some mathematics developed by some  Polish uh  mathematicians to break this Enigma code  and he is now known as the father of uh  the modern computer and one of his  quotes was I propose to consider the  question can machines think  well that was the 1940s and we've come a  long way since the 1940s  in 2022 a device worn around our wrists  carries exponentially more Computing  capability than devices that required  large cooled rooms in the 1950s  we now are having self-driving Vehicles  facial and voice recognition and the  ability to connect classrooms worldwide  and also the integration of human  cognition with the rapid processing of  artificial intelligence and these are  all examples of these major advances  that have taken place in these last few  decades  in medicine  we've also made considerable progress  now here this slide shows three eras of  medicine the past present and future  so in the past  we relied largely on intuition medicine  so for example in my field in  neonatology  we think that premature babies have  immature immune systems so we have to  use antibiotics a lot in these babies  and in fact antibiotics are given to  almost every premature baby weighing  less than uh 1500 grams in most neonatal  intensive care units in the world  but this is intuition based we don't  know if this is really the right thing  to do  in the present we are now undergoing  some uh associative studies uh uh  epidemiologic studies and also  prospective randomized trials that are  hopefully telling us whether this is a  good practice or not  in the future  we hope to be able to use Precision  medicine in other words  be able to get information from each  individual patient and be able to tell  whether that patient should be getting  antibiotics or not and this will be an  algorithmic based approach  so here are some of the different modes  of artificial intelligence we have  Predictive Analytics which rely on a  technique called machine learning we  have uh text to speech and speech to  text we have image recognition and uh at  the bottom we see additional forms of  artificial intelligence which are just  in the uh very early stages of being  applied to Medicine  in terms of Publications  related to Medicine  when we talk about multiomics and  artificial intelligence this is a slide  that shows a logarithmic scale and the  increase in various Publications in  these areas over the past uh 12 or so  years  so marked increase in interest and  Publications in these areas  now here's a picture of a type of a baby  that I take care of in the neonatal  Intensive Care Unit  and this is a fertile area for  artificial intelligence and multi-omic  applications  for example we want to be able to use  predictive analytic analytics  Diagnostics especially when coupled with  multiomics and also Precision  nutritional support  and I want to give you just some  examples of these uh applications  now here's a fellow who you might  recognize he is now 72 years old but  when I was growing up this was little  Stevie Wonder he was a child prodigy and  a musician and he had some very famous  songs and he's wearing sunglasses and  the reason he's wearing sunglasses is  not to be cool  reason he's wearing sunglasses is  because he is blind  he is blind because he has a disease  called retinopathy of prematurity  retinopathy of prematurity was first  recognized in the late 1940s  in an increased suddenly in premature  infants in the late 1940s  in the early 1950s it was found to be  associated with routine High oxygen  exposure enclosed in incubators  it was initially called retro lentil  fibroplasia  but as more was learned the name changed  to retinopathy of prematurity  if you look closely at the picture at  the top here you see that this baby has  very cloudy looking uh uh eyes the uh  pupils of the eyes look cloudy the  reason for that is because the retinas  have detached and that causes this  cloudy looking appearance and once the  retina is detach from the backs of the  eyes the baby is likely to be blind  now  we have some newer techniques utilizing  artificial intelligence that help us in  terms of uh diagnosis of this  retinopathy of prematurity  we are able to look at pictures of the  backs of the eyes  and rather than having different  ophthalmologists have a different  impression of what the retina looks like  we now have algorithms that really tell  us with a high degree of accuracy the  stage of retinopathy of prematurity  and to look at the features in the backs  of the eyes and the retina using these  techniques is actually beginning to be  more accurate than the diagnosis made by  the ophthalmologist  we also have a terrible disease entity  that we see in premature babies I should  call this a diagnosis rather than a  disease and this is uh called  necrotizing enterocolitis and here is a  picture of a baby on the top left that  has a distended abdomen and you see a  little bit of uh redness around the  belly button this baby had x-ray taken  and the two arrows are pointing to  features that are common in this disease  the bottom Arrow points to air in the  bowel wall and the top Arrow points to  air in the liver  this may be continued to do worse and  worse and then was taken to surgery and  at the bottom left you can see what this  Baby's intestine looked like at surgery  the arrow points to a necrotic piece of  intestine and that was resected and the  baby did uh did well after the reception  however  about 20 to 30 percent of the babies  with uh the surgical form of necrotizing  enterocolitis end up dying of this  disease  we also are beginning to recognize as I  mentioned before that this is uh uh a  diagnosis and not a discrete clinical  entity we do not have a good definition  of this disease at this time and we lump  several different entities under the  umbrella term of necrotizing  intercollitus  we have not made very much progress  because of this and we also have animal  models that have been studied that  really don't represent the disease that  we see in humans and we've had narrow  focus on individual Pathways rather than  systems and so we have not made very  much progress in this uh uh with uh  helping predict this diagnosis or uh  preventing it  here are just some of the imposters of  what we are calling necrotizing in our  colitis and so there are several  different entities that fall under the  umbrella here  work that we did at the University of  Florida uh just in the past couple of  years  involved uh looking at babies who have  uh uh what  we uh consider to be spontaneous  intestinal perforation versus the true  necrosis of the intestine  and on this slide we see that using  machine learning techniques  we have been working on trying to be  able to utilize features of these two  different entities that are called  necrotizing enterocolitis  uh to be able to tell whether this is  true necrotizing enterocolitis versus  spontaneous intestinal perforation  before the surgeon actually goes in and  takes a look  and so  uh  this slide  shows the uh uh baby with the  spontaneous intestinal perforation and  here again is the more classic form of  necrotizing and our colitis the way we  studied this was to take a large number  of babies who had a exploratory  laparotomies and we were able to take 40  patients who actually underwent the  laparotomy and clearly showed surgical  necrosis of the intestine versus the  spontaneous intestinal perforation and  then using  um  machine learning we were able to find  certain features  as seen at the bottom right here certain  features that fit more with one form of  this uh uh problem versus the other form  of the problem and so we did two  different kinds of machine learning uh  models and both of these machine  learning models were able to separate uh  the necrotizing enterocolitis the true  necrotizing enterocolitis from the  spontaneous intestinal perforation with  a high degree of certainty  so what does this mean  this means that rather than going in and  operating to try to find out what this  baby actually has  the surgeon can predict with a high  degree of accuracy whether this is a  spontaneous intestinal perforation or  necrotizing enterocolitis for  spontaneous intestinal perforation it is  easier to just put a little drain into  the abdominal cavity rather than doing a  full laparotomy and that drain will then  drain the uh peritoneal cavity and the  little hole will heal over and the baby  should do fine with just that procedure  relatively simple procedure rather than  opening the abdominal cavity completely  so this can be very helpful for the  Pediatric surgeon to discern these two  different forms of intestinal injury  next I'd like to talk a little bit more  about multiomics  and if we talk about omics probably the  first omic that we think about is the  genome or genomics and uh uh genomics is  something that has burgeoned over the  past several decades we now are able to  uh evaluate our human genome uh very  rapidly we can uh some of the  Laboratories that do human genomic  sequencing are able to uh get uh DNA  from an individual and they're able to  sequence this and provide the uh  sequence in less than a day so  tremendous progress has been done has  been made in genomics  but there are other forms of omics  one of these forms is called the  microbiome  and this has stemmed from work in  genomics some of the technology  developed in the Human Genome Project  has been applied to uh microbial DNA  sequencing an RNA sequencing and we have  several different forms of  microbiome sequencing Technologies one  of these is called 16s sequencing which  looks at a small portion of the  microbial Genome of 15 1500 nuclear base  region and this is very helpful in  telling us the types of microbes that  are there the relative abundance of  microbes that are there the diversity of  the microbes so it can be very helpful  to some extent but it doesn't tell us  what the uh actual potential the  physiologic potential of those microbes  are it just tells us uh their relative  abundance and who might be there whole  genome sequencing on the other hand  looks at the entire microbial genome and  the databases that are available for  whole genome sequencing can tell us much  more about what the actual potential of  these uh microbes are what they are  doing in the uh uh Niche that they are  in  so we have applied microbiome sequencing  to evaluate necrotizing enterocolitis  and the types of microbes that are  present prior to the development of uh  this diagnosis  and here we see these two graphs at the  top and the graph at the left represents  a time period with babies at 24 25 to 36  weeks gestation and the colors represent  a different phyla of bacteria  these are the control babies and on the  right we see the necrotizing and our  colitis babies the NEC babies  and one can see that the NEC babies over  time from 24 to 35 weeks if we sequence  their microbiome we see differences  compared to the controls the Blue  appears to increase these are a certain  volume of bacteria called the  proteobacteria  the yellow the dark yellow begins to  decrease  and these are bacteria called the  bacterides  we know that uh the proteobacteria are  bacteria that have a lot of uh  lipopolysaccharide this is a uh an agent  in their cell walls that stimulates an  inflammatory response and so we are  seeing more of these uh potentially  pathogenic microbes develop prior to the  diagnosis of this disease and then the  uh light or the dark yellow represents  the firmicutes and or I'm sorry the yes  the firmicutes and these firmicutes  produce  a certain  types of short chain fatty acids that  are known to be protective in the  gastrointestinal tract so this is  telling us that there is an association  between the types of microbes in the  gastrointestinal tract of these babies  before the development of the disease  however  this is an association  and it doesn't tell us whether this is  actually causing the disease so when  studies like this find an association  between two things it does not mean that  one thing caused the other to happen  so how can we get by this and this is  where multiomics comes in  and I really like to explain multi-omics  using this Parable and it's the parable  of the six Blind Men of Hindustan  each one of these Blind Men of Hindustan  was asked to feel this structure  and one of the Blind Men went to the  side of this structure and said this  feels like a wall  the other blind man went to uh the back  of the structure and said this feels  like a rope uh the other blind man went  to the uh front of the animal or in  front of this structure and said this  feels like a spear and so forth and all  these uh Blind Men came back and they  were dogmatic in what they felt  they didn't talk to one another  they didn't really collaborate  and this was problematic because they  were not able to determine that this was  truly an elephant  had they talked to one another  collaborated they would have been able  to tell if this was an elephant and this  is where multi-omics comes in  and individually multi each one of these  omics for example the genome can be very  helpful  but you can have  genomic mutations and genomic variations  which are never truly expressed unless  you have certain environmental  conditions that come in and cause that  genomic uh sequence to be expressed and  then you can see that in the  transcriptome and the proteome in the  metabolum and so one can look at these  different omics under different  conditions over time and integrate them  and they can much more strongly identify  patterns associated with disease and  even causative of a disease or outcome  and once we have this this can lead more  readily lead to a preventative or  therapeutic targets  here  see how different layers of these omics  are placed on one another and you can  start with a phenotype or you can start  with a gene a genome first uh techniques  and you put them together and you find  this uh pattern that can develop with  these different nodes that we see these  circles and colors and uh the larger  they are the the larger these are in  this mechanistic modeling the greater is  the interaction  and so using the systems biology  integration of omics data  we can now  much more readily look at  uh the uh these uh omics and be able to  uh evaluate mechanisms of disease  so here is just one example  uh inflammatory bowel disease and this  is a study that was published a few  years ago to evaluate what happens when  you look at single omics with single  omics you can get secondary analyzes as  I mentioned before you will get uh  associations but if you put these omics  together and you do you integrate them  you get primary mechanisms so much much  better than uh uh when you look at a  single omics approach in terms of  defining mechanisms of the disease  so here we get back to this little  premature baby a 25-week gestation  intrauterine growth restricted female  this is telling us that this baby  probably had poor nutrition in utero and  how do we nourish this baby  well in the past using the Paradigm that  I mentioned before we would say well we  have these uh uh  formulas available for premature babies  that can be used but all of the formulas  are pretty much the same for premature  babies we have uh Mother's Milk which  could be used  this doesn't tell us very much about uh  that particular baby and what is best  for that particular baby  we're currently undergoing all kinds of  studies to try to use evidence-based  medicine to provide information on how  to best nourish a baby like this but in  the future  we will be able to say this was an  intrauterine growth restricted baby this  is a girl this is a baby who had an  infection at Birth and put all those  features together and be able to tell  what is the most appropriate nutrition  for that particular baby  we are beginning to recognize that  precision and nutrition is something  that is very important because we do not  all respond the same to the same food  intake  this is a study that was published in  nature medicine a couple of years ago  where they looked at identical twins and  gave these identical twins very similar  diets and then they looked at  triglyceride  glucose responses various nutritional  responses and they found that these  twins had different responses to the  same nutrients  but they found that there was a very  high correlation to the type of microbes  present in the gastrointestinal tract of  these twins and the uh  responses  were highly uh correlated to the types  of microbes present in the  gastrointestinal tract  so when it comes to nutrition  why personalized well here we have two  different types of premature babies a 23  week pre-term and a 29 week pre-term and  the type of nutrition we need to give  these babies is very different because  one size does not fit all and we should  be able to prevent some of the very  adverse outcomes that we see in these  babies by providing better nutrition  from these babies for these babies early  on  now  we can provide different forms of  formulas to these uh babies we can  provide a baby's own mother's milk or we  can provide donor milk  baked donor milk to these babies but we  are beginning to recognize that baby's  own Mother's Milk provides for the  greatest Precision Nutrition and on this  slide we see results of a study that  were published in 2011 where 16  different uh  milk 16 different Mothers Milk samples  were taken at three different time  periods and each one of these  um represents uh each one of these s's  represents a different mother  and  uh we have these Columns of colors that  represent one week two weeks and four  weeks after birth and we see that the  colors differ markedly from one mother  to the other  so the composition the microbial  composition which represents the colors  that we see here are very different from  one mother to the next  so this is suggesting that the types of  microbes that we find in mother's milk  may be uh very much uh  Precision oriented for that particular  baby  why do I say that  in terms of personalization uh the  baby's own mother's milk over time is uh  considered to be a highly personalized  uh type of nutrition and one of the  reasons is seen here something called  the intro memory immune system where the  mother and the baby if they have close  contact with one another the baby's  immune system may not be very well yet  developed yet but the mothers is and so  what happens is that any microbes that  the baby is  affected or colonized with will get into  the mother's mouth by kissing the baby  or being very close to the baby and that  will get into the mother's  gastrointestinal tract where her mucosal  immune system sets up in an immune  response  that then gets transmitted to the milk  and the baby will uh drink that mother's  milk and takes take in some of the  immunoglobulins that are specific for  that particular uh microbe that the uh  baby may be colonized by so this is one  of the uh rationales for uh seeing  different types of microbes in different  samples of milk again this says not yet  been uh fully tested uh in in terms of  rigorous studies but it seems to uh be a  very good strong hypothesis that needs  further testing  uh work that we have done uh in our  Laboratories uh two pictures here uh Dr  uh Nicole Cacho who is a postdoctoral  fellow at the time and Dr Graziano Lorca  at the bottom she is a uh professor of  microbiology at the University of  Florida we did some studies looking at  the personalization of the microbiota of  donor human milk with the mother's own  milk well donor human milk comes from  lots of mothers and it is pasteurized in  the uh microbes are killed by this  pasteurization process and that may  actually be problematic in donor milk  that you are not providing microbes  and these microbes may actually be very  helpful so one of the things we did in  uh this study that was published in  Frontiers in microbiology in 2017 was to  take small amounts of the baby's own  Mother's Milk put that into donor milk  and uh allow that to incubate for  different periods of time and we found  that the incubation period between four  to eight hours resulted in a uh  refination of the donor milk and a  profile of microbes that look very much  the same as the baby's own mother's milk  we also looked at the metabolites and  found that the metabolites uh seen here  in these uh principal component plots  that uh we were able to make the  metabolites look much  more like the baby's own mother's milk  if they were uh provided with some of  the a small amount of the uh mother's  milk into the uh donor milk larger donor  milk samples  so this is telling us that uh we may be  able to do some personalization and this  is a  one of the pathways toward  personalization of uh donor milk in the  future  now how do we currently monitor  nutrition in our premature babies well  one way is to use these growth curves as  seen here on the chart and we can see  that uh we should be following these  growth curves but these dark lines  represent growth faltering  this means that the baby is not growing  the way the baby should  problematic  because once we see this kind of growth  faltering it is probably already late in  the kind of damage that this growth  faltering can cause for this Baby's  entire lifetime can be problematic  we need good indicators that help  prevent this  the growth curves are gaussian  distributions and they are certainly not  personalized so we need to do something  to be able to personalize nutrition and  this is something that we are starting  to work on uh using artificial  intelligence for prediction of growth  failure and here's a reference that was  recently published in a biomedical  bioinformatics and uh here we developed  a model that integrates clinical and  microbiome data  and the clinical and microbiome data was  derived from a large British cohort and  three cohorts in the United States and  we compare different models to uh  uh get uh data on what would be the best  clinical predictors of growth faltering  and we also found that uh information on  the kind of taxonomy in the uh of  microbes in the gastrointestinal tract  may actually be helpful for us to  predict this growth faltering and so  this told us that we need to move  further and perhaps even take a  multi-ohmic approach uh in uh being able  to do this so what kind of studies do we  have planned in the future  Well we'd like to be able to get timed  infant samples from Theses saliva blood  and urine and then look at the  microbiome look at immune and  inflammatory markers and metabolomics  and proteomics and put them together  into this network analysis and then be  able to uh  delineate the different diseases that  might develop or  adverse outcomes that might develop  depending on the type of nutrition we  can then also do biomarker Discovery and  then this could potentially lead to  personalized therapeutic interventions  we still have a long ways to go with  this but I think that the tools are  there  so  here's a summary and where we are going  in the future  the causal nature of single omic  associations are not well understood but  they are amenable to analysis by newly  developing Technologies such as  multiomics and artificial intelligence  once these mechanisms are understood we  will better be able to provide  Predictive Analytics and personalized  nutrition  for mothers and infants  and in order to accomplish this we need  to have teams of scientists we need to  have clinicians basic scientists  engineers and bioinformaticians working  together and communicating and working  as a team to be able to uh move forward  and have positive accomplishments in the  these areas thank you for your attention  and thank you Dr Noy for that time today  and your important research and before  we go I want to thank our audience for  joining us today as well questions  submitted today and during the on-demand  period will be addressed by our speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration and this  webcast can be viewed on demand for two  years until September 6 2024. lab routes  will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time take care everyone  bye-bye

